<BaseLayout title="DFIR Homelab" description="A controlled environment for practical digital forensics, incident response, monitoring, and detection engineering." currentPath="projects">
  <main class="page-shell">
    <article class="archive-panel markdown-page">
      <p class="record-label">Cybersecurity Project</p>
      <div class="markdown-content">
        <h1>DFIR Homelab</h1>
        <p class="lede">A controlled, self-hosted environment for building and demonstrating practical digital forensics and incident response capabilities.</p>

        <h2>Overview</h2>
        <p>The DFIR Homelab brings together monitored endpoints, attacker and victim systems, centralised logging, and supporting security services in a controlled environment. It provides a repeatable platform for security monitoring, attack simulation, investigation, and evidence collection.</p>

        <h2>Purpose</h2>
        <p>The lab is designed to develop practical skills in digital forensics, incident response, detection engineering, network analysis, and security monitoring. It also provides a place to validate security controls and document complete investigation workflows.</p>

        <h2>Architecture</h2>
        <p>The environment uses virtualised attacker and Windows victim systems alongside monitoring and case-management services. Endpoint and Sysmon telemetry is collected for central analysis, while network separation is used to keep laboratory activity controlled.</p>

        <h2>Core Technologies</h2>
        <ul class="technology-grid">
          <li>Proxmox</li>
          <li>Ludus</li>
          <li>Wazuh</li>
          <li>DFIR-IRIS</li>
          <li>Windows victim systems</li>
          <li>Attacker systems</li>
          <li>Sysmon and endpoint telemetry</li>
          <li>Atomic Red Team</li>
        </ul>

        <h2>Current Capabilities</h2>
        <ul>
          <li>Docker-based Wazuh deployment for central log collection, endpoint visibility, and alerting</li>
          <li>Windows endpoint monitoring with the Wazuh agent and Sysmon telemetry</li>
          <li>Pi-hole DNS filtering and DNS activity visibility</li>
          <li>Virtual machines for controlled attacker, victim, and investigation roles</li>
          <li>A foundation for segmented laboratory and monitoring networks</li>
        </ul>

        <h2>Current Work / Next Steps</h2>
        <ul>
          <li>Complete the isolated laboratory network and validate segmentation rules</li>
          <li>Develop reusable attacker and victim systems through Ludus</li>
          <li>Forward and validate endpoint and network telemetry in Wazuh</li>
          <li>Use controlled Atomic Red Team techniques to generate repeatable evidence</li>
          <li>Document investigations and incident workflows in DFIR-IRIS</li>
        </ul>
      </div>
    </article>
  </main>
</BaseLayout>
